+### Declare notation at the point of use
+
+Read each section and subsection as a local unit. Before its first formula or result, state the ambient objects and the hypotheses needed there. For example, a definition of $\PSH(X,\theta)$ must first identify $X$ as a complex manifold and $\theta$ as a smooth real $(1,1)$-form; compactness, connectedness or a chosen Hermitian form must be stated when the section uses them. Do not infer an object's type from its letter or silently import stronger assumptions from a neighboring section.
+
+An inherited setting can be recalled in one precise sentence: name the actual objects and essential hypotheses, then cite the earlier definition for a specialized construction. “We retain the notation above” alone is not an adequate declaration when the reader must search to learn what those objects are. Recall only the setting used locally; avoid duplicating entire definitions or adding a decorative notation environment.
+
+Within statements and proofs, introduce every new map with its domain and codomain, every measure with its space, every sequence or family with its index range, and every constant or auxiliary parameter with its permitted dependence when relevant. Bound variables may be introduced by the quantifier, sum, integral or formula that binds them. Define reused operators, classes and shorthand before applying them; standard universal notation such as real numbers does not need to be redefined. A change in the meaning of a symbol starts a new scope and must be announced.
+
+For a manuscript-wide revision, keep a coverage record for every active section and subsection. List each local setting, first-use problem and repair; inspect the complete statements and proofs as well as the opening paragraph. Cross-check every added declaration against the previous hypotheses and proof so that an exposition repair neither strengthens a claim nor adds an unproved assumption. Do not certify absence of undeclared notation from a token scanner: use such scans only to locate candidates for mathematical reading.
+
